// How an application's window is presented.
//
// Two questions wear the same vocabulary. `GraphicsApplication.windowMode` is
// the mode the window OPENS in, which is what a library shipping an application
// shell sets as its default; `RenderFrame.setWindowMode` is the mode a running
// program moves to, which is what a menu item or a keyboard shortcut drives.
// Both name a state rather than flipping a flag, so "go fullscreen" and "come
// back to a window" are the same call with a different argument and neither
// caller has to know which state the window is in first.
//
// `Windowed` is a decorated, resizable window at the size the application asked
// for. `WindowedBorderless` is the same window with its title bar and frame
// removed. `Fullscreen` covers the display the window is on, and the size the
// application asked for stops applying — read `viewportWidth`/`viewportHeight`
// off the frame rather than assuming it was honoured.
//
// A backend that has one undecorated window and no separate borderless state
// says so by what `windowMode` reads back: see `sokolWindowMode`.
enum WindowMode {
    Windowed
    WindowedBorderless
    Fullscreen
}
